\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We validate through Monte Carlo simulation ($K{=}3$), cross-$K$ robustness checks ($K{=}5,7$), and empirical validation with real LLM confusion matrices from three NLP tasks ($K{=}2,3,10$).

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

For each topology, we sample 100{,}000 confusion matrices from a Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, compute analytical probability limits, and validate against Monte Carlo simulation ($N{=}10{,}000$). ASV experiments use $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$.

\subsection{Bias Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy_results}

\paragraph{Theory-MC agreement.}
Analytical probability limits match Monte Carlo estimates with grand MAPE of 1.6\% across 161 scenarios (23 LLM configurations $\times$ 7 topologies; per-topology range: 0.3\%--3.7\%).

\paragraph{Exposure DAG: sign reversal under multi-class misclassification.}
Under realistic LLM accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), the violation rate is 13.7\% with 2.1\% sign reversal; even at diagonal $\geq 0.9$, violation persists at 11.9\%. Under the full Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, 85\% of $K{=}3$ confusion matrices violate the binary ``toward-null'' guarantee, and 83.4\% cause sign reversal of at least one coefficient. The $K{=}2$ control confirms that all tested binary matrices maintain classical attenuation~\cite{brenner1993}, establishing that the multi-class failure is structurally distinct.

\paragraph{Structural guarantee for non-exposure topologies.}
Five topologies exhibit zero sign-flip rate (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}): confounding, mediation, and front-door show 100\% amplification~\cite{brenner1993, dosemeci1990} but never reverse the sign; collider is protective ($|B(C)|$ non-increasing; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}); M-bias shows weakest sensitivity (53.2\% violation). All patterns persist at $K{=}5,7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_k_robustness}).

\paragraph{IV: Wald estimator instability.}
The IV topology is the sole non-exposure exception: 52.6\% of random matrices cause sign reversal, stable across $K{=}3,5,7$. Diagonal dominance ($C_{kk} > 0.8$) reduces but does not eliminate IV reversal (Appendix~\ref{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}); however, all 23 empirical LLM configurations show zero IV sign reversal.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Bias under $K{=}3$ non-differential misclassification (100{,}000 Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ matrices). Violation: $|\text{bias}| > 0$; Sign Flip: any coefficient reverses sign; Amplif.: $|\hat{\tau}| > |\tau|$. $\tau_{\text{sf}}{=}0$?: sign-flip guarantee holds unconditionally (Yes), conditionally (Cond.; Proposition~\ref{prop:signflip}), or not (No). Collider 100\% amplification reflects Berkson bias---misclassification is protective.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Topology} & \textbf{Violation} & \textbf{Sign Flip} & \textbf{Amplif.} & $\tau_{\text{sf}}{=}0$? \\
\midrule
Confounding & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & Cond. \\
Exposure & 85\% & 83.4\% & 14.1\% & No \\
Mediation & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & Cond. \\
Front-door & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & Cond. \\
Collider & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & Cond. \\
M-bias & 53.2\% & 0\% & 10.4\% & Yes \\
IV & 100\% & 52.6\% & 100\% & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The bias distributions are visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:bias_distribution}.

\subsection{ASV Framework Validation}
\label{sec:asv_results}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_asv_ranking.pdf}
\caption{ASV vulnerability ranking across topologies. Three ASV types (magnitude, significance, sign-flip) reveal a topology-dependent spectrum from IV (maximally fragile) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$).}
\label{fig:asv_ranking}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{ASV results.}
The ASV envelope is $\varepsilon$-approximately monotonic for non-IV topologies ($\varepsilon < 0.008$ for non-exposure, $\varepsilon \approx 0.075$ for exposure; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}). Figure~\ref{fig:asv_ranking} shows the vulnerability ranking: IV is maximally fragile, M-bias is immune ($\text{ASV} = \infty$). Sign-flip ASV: 83.4\% of exposure matrices yield $\delta^*_{\text{sf}} < 1$; IV ranges 1.1--6.7 (Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid}). Significance ASV exhibits a phase transition at $N^* \approx 6{,}000$--$10{,}000$; bootstrap CIs ($B{=}1{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{sec:bootstrap_asv}) confirm stable topology ranking despite wide IV intervals.

\subsection{Empirical Validation}
\label{sec:empirical}

We validate using real LLM confusion matrices from HumAID~\cite{alam2021humaid} ($K{=}10$), VAST ($K{=}3$), and CivilComments ($K{=}2$), with four LLMs producing 23 configurations under zero-shot and few-shot prompting (161 bias scenarios).

\paragraph{Empirical bias ranking.}
HumAID ($K{=}10$) matches predictions: IV (12.4--42.1\%) and exposure (9.0--17.4\%) dominate; remaining topologies $<$15\%. Across 56 HumAID scenarios, $F_1$ and $|\text{bias}|$ show no significant rank correlation (Spearman $\rho = -0.10$, $p = 0.45$; Figure~\ref{fig:f1_vs_bias}). Of $K{-}1$ exposure coefficients, 14/23 configurations (61\%) exhibit sign reversal---all secondary; zero across 138 non-exposure scenarios.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_f1_vs_bias.pdf}
\caption{Topology determines downstream causal bias, not $F_1$. HumAID $K{=}10$ (56 points): within $F_1$ range 0.614--0.690, bias varies $10{\times}$ (IV 42.1\% vs.\ collider $<$5.6\%).}
\label{fig:f1_vs_bias}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods (naive plug-in, MLA, DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023}) across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations; full results in Table~\ref{tab:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
Under linear OLS, FWL implies that any linear transformation of $\mathbf{D}^*$ preserves the column space relative to $T$ for the four covariate topologies, so naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction (Appendix~\ref{app:fwl_proof}); exposure and IV are excluded. Under logistic regression the invariance breaks ($|\Delta\,\text{SF}_\tau| < 0.02$; Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp},~\ref{sec:fwl_nonlinear}).

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
At $K{=}3$, PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean RMSE reduction (47.0\%) but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%): misclassification attenuates the spurious M-bias path, and PPI++ removes this protective attenuation. MC-SIMEX is more $K$-robust (27--70\% reduction across $K{=}3,5,10$) and gold-free, though it harms front-door ($-$62.4\%). Rankings are stable across $K$ (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.80$; Appendix~\ref{sec:correction_k_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Correction decision procedure.}
Given DAG topology $\mathcal{T}$ and gold-label availability: (1)~\textbf{Risk:} M-bias $\to$ low; confounding, mediation, collider, front-door $\to$ medium (no sign reversal); exposure, IV $\to$ high. (2)~\textbf{Method:} with gold labels, use PPI++ (47.0\% RMSE reduction) except M-bias (harmful, $-$88\%); without gold, DSL for confounding/mediation, MC-SIMEX for IV/collider, otherwise acquire gold. (3)~\textbf{FWL warning:} skip naive plug-in and MLA for covariate topologies (0\% bias reduction). If $\mathcal{T}$ is uncertain, repeat under candidate topologies (full pseudocode: Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{Case studies and sensitivity.}
ClinicalBERT validation confirms FWL invariance in clinical NLP (Appendix~\ref{app:case_study}). PPI++ requires ${\geq}20\%$ gold for IV but harms M-bias ($-125\%$ at 5\% gold; Appendix~\ref{sec:ppi_gold_ratio}). Under logistic regression, all structural patterns---sign preservation, topology ranking, FWL invariance breakdown---persist with deviations $< 0.02$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp},~\ref{sec:fwl_nonlinear}).

